First, let's understand the chemical reactions involved:

1. Compound B (dimer of lactic acid) hydrolyzes to produce lactic acid (\ce{C3H6O3}).
2. Lactic acid reacts with \ce{NaOH} to form sodium lactate (\ce{C3H5O3Na}) and water (\ce{H2O}).
3. Any excess \ce{NaOH} is titrated with \ce{HCl}.

Given:
- Volume of sample solution: \pu{20.00 cm^3}
- Volume of \ce{NaOH} solution added: \pu{40.00 cm^3}
